{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And she called his name, etc., and she said, etc.</b> Now you will find a difference in the naming of Yitzchak and Yaakov and the tribes, for in all of them the reason for the name precedes and afterward the name itself. Regarding Yitzchak (Genesis 21:6): ״כָּל הַשּׁוֹמֵעַ יִצְחַק וְגוֹ׳ וַתִּקְרָא שְׁמוֹ יִצְחָק״; Yaakov (Genesis 25:26): ״וְיָדוֹ אוֹחֶזֶת בַּעֲקֵב וַיִּקְרָא שְׁמוֹ יַעֲקֹב״. Regarding the tribes: ״כִּי רָאָה ה׳ וַתִּקְרָא שְׁמוֹ רְאוּבֵן״ (Genesis 29:32), ״כִּי שָׁמַע וְגוֹ׳ וַתִּקְרָא שְׁמוֹ שִׁמְעוֹן״ (Genesis 29:33), and so on in this manner. But this is not so in the naming of Moshe, where the mention of his name preceded and afterward the reason for the name." "Or perhaps it is because the matriarchs possessed ruach ha-kodesh, and they would grasp the spiritual dimension of the name, and the calling of the name corresponded to what they intended by it — whereas this was not so with the naming of Moshe. The verse indicates that she did not grasp knowledge of the spiritual dimension of the name, for his name tells wonders. Go and learn from what is written in Sefer ha-Zohar (vol. 3, p. 276; Tikkunim, Tikkun 69) what this name alludes to. And YHWH — HaShem is His name — placed in the earth; He placed in her mouth the name Moshe, and what she herself said as the reason for the name is <b>כִּי מִן הַמַּיִם מְשִׁיתִהוּ</b>, and she knew no reason other than this." "<b>Or</b> the verse may intend to say that this woman concealed the reason for the name so that it would not be revealed to all, because the deed was contrary to the will of all her people — to save them, for they had decreed to cast them into the river, and she would nullify their counsel to their harm. Therefore she did not publicize the reason for the name but called it simply, without a reason, and the verse informs us of her reason for calling him so, namely because she said ״כִּי מִן הַמַּיִם״."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "The phrase 'וַה׳ הַשָּׂם שֵׁמוֹת בָּאָרֶץ שָׂם בְּפִיהָ שֵׁם מֹשֶׁה' appears to be a poetic wordplay on שָׂם/שֵׁם/שֵׁמוֹת; rendered hyper-literally as the syntax is elliptical and possibly stylistic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:38.941728760Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}